<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The app's own privacy manifest. Third-party SDKs ship their own; this file
  covers the Runner binary, which INCLUDES all compiled Dart — a distinction
  that decides what belongs here.

  NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes is deliberately EMPTY, and that was measured rather
  than copied. The four categories most Flutter apps declare are covered by
  someone else or not reached at all:

    · UserDefaults  — reached only through shared_preferences, and
                      `shared_preferences_foundation` ships its OWN manifest.
    · Disk space    — nothing in our Dart or in flutter_cache_manager queries it.
    · File timestamp— flutter_cache_manager is pure Dart, so it WOULD be ours to
                      declare; it decides freshness from its own sqflite
                      metadata (`validTill`), not from File.lastModified().
    · sqlite        — sqflite_darwin ships its own manifest.

  Over-declaring is not free: Apple asks for the APIs actually called, and a
  declaration nobody can point at a caller for is a claim we cannot defend on
  review. If a future dependency reaches one of these from Dart, add it here
  WITH the caller named.

  NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is intentionally absent — see
  docs/design/mobile-store-submission.md §4. The authoritative declaration of
  what MyWeli collects is the App Store Connect privacy questionnaire, and it
  must be answered by the owner; a second copy here that drifts from it is worse
  than none.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- No tracking, and no data linked to third-party advertising. MyWeli has
	     no ad SDK, no analytics SDK and no attribution SDK. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
